WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
ai just design brand new proteins to fight deadly scorpion venom. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 203. Privacy locks in ChatGPT, a helper that can read several open tabs, Gemini in the car, and a deep dive are up after the lead. Andthropics Claude, an artificial intelligence assistant, created hundreds of thousands of protein designs, meant to stick to scorpion neurotoxins and shut them down, the decoder reports. A neurotoxin is a venom that attacks your nerves. The bodies

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.960
wiring that carries messages. Researchers then tested 211 of those designs in real labs.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.440
17 of them actually grabbed onto the Venom proteins the way the team hoped.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.040
The toxins came from scorpions in Mexico, North Africa, and the Middle East.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:54.130
They included CN2 from a Mexican scorpion, TS1 from a Brazilian species, AAH2 and AAH1 from North

00:00:54.130 --> 00:01:00.910
African species, an LQHII from a Middle Eastern species, picture a lock on a nerve cell

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:05.090
and the venom as a stolen key that jams the lock and stops the cell from working.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.790
Claude was inventing brand new keys from scratch, shaped to block that stolen key, instead

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.010
of copying keys that already exist in nature.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:19.370
Two contract research organizations, Adaptive Bio and Twist Bio-Science, measured how well

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:20.430
those new keys stuck.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:25.990
A contract research organization is an outside lab you hire to run careful tests for you.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:31.810
They use surface plasma resonance, or SPR, a lab technique that tracks how tightly one

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.110
molecule sticks to another.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.470
Think of SPR as a super sensitive handshake meter.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.130
It does not just ask whether two pieces touch.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.190
It asks how hard they hold on.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.970
Cloud did not throw random shapes at the wall.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.590
It used what it knows about how molecules fit together to propose shapes that might stop

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.390
the toxins from harming nerve cells.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.830
Developing medicines or antidotes usually takes years of trial and error in labs, like

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.290
tasting thousands of recipes before one works.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.870
Tools like this could speed up the early idea stage dramatically.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.330
The part where scientists decide what is even worth mixing in a real test tube.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:12.090
If you care about biology or medicine, this is AI as a lab partner, not just a homework

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:12.450
helper.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.850
Future doctors and researchers could use similar tools to tackle rare diseases or new health

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.470
threats faster. These designs are still early-stage research and not ready as medicine, and that

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.930
honesty matters. The full set of tested designs and lab results has been released publicly so

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:34.530
other scientists can build on them. The data lives on hugging face, a public library of research files

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:40.810
under the data set name andthropic scorpion toxin binder design. The technical report is titled

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:46.630
Denovo Design of Protein Binders against scorpion toxins with Claude, and Denovo just means

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:52.090
design from scratch. You can read that report on Anthropics site and you can open the data set on

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:58.215
hugging face right now. Search for Anthropics, Scorpion, Tox, and Binder design. Then look at the

00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:03.435
spreadsheet of sequences and the stickiness numbers from both labs. You do not need to understand

00:03:03.435 --> 00:03:08.975
every column on the first pass. Just notice that a computer's guesses became real molecules that

00:03:08.975 --> 00:03:14.415
real instruments could measure. A protein is a long chain built from 20 kinds of building blocks

00:03:14.415 --> 00:03:19.235
called amino acids. Picture those building blocks as beads on a friendship bracelet.

00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:24.255
The order of the beads decides what shape the chain folds into, and the shape decides

00:03:24.255 --> 00:03:29.715
what job it can do. Your phone already tries to finish your sentences when you text, based

00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:34.475
on what you typed and what usually comes next. Claude did a version of that guessing game,

00:03:34.695 --> 00:03:40.155
except the words were amino acids, and the sentence was a whole protein. It studied the shape

00:03:40.155 --> 00:03:45.195
of the scorpion toxin, then proposed huge numbers of bracelet patterns that might nest against

00:03:45.195 --> 00:03:50.315
that toxin like a puzzle piece. Then it trimmed the list using rules about how amino acids

00:03:50.315 --> 00:03:55.915
like to sit together or keep apart. The way classmates pick seats at lunch. That filter is

00:03:55.915 --> 00:04:01.275
similar to your phone refusing to suggest nonsense words. None of the working designs existed in

00:04:01.275 --> 00:04:07.295
nature before. The AI invented them on its own. Then humans put the best candidates into actual lab

00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:13.395
equipment. The ones that worked got short names such as CN2B1, like code names for new

00:04:13.395 --> 00:04:18.235
characters in a game. The public spreadsheet lists each design's bead by bead sequence,

00:04:18.535 --> 00:04:23.675
plus the binding measurements from both labs. Binding just means sticking, the way two magnets

00:04:23.675 --> 00:04:29.435
click or fail to click. So when someone says an AI designed a protein, you can picture a bracelet

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:34.495
maker who has seen countless bead patterns, studies one lock, and proposes new bracelets

00:04:34.495 --> 00:04:39.935
that might fit it. The lab is still the boss. The model suggests the instruments decide.

00:04:40.475 --> 00:04:44.935
Once you can see those two jobs as separate, the whole story feels a lot less like magic

00:04:44.935 --> 00:04:49.035
and a lot more like a very fast sketch artist handing ideas to a careful builder.

00:04:50.094 --> 00:04:55.694
Open AI is rolling out lock chats and chat GPT. A way to move sensitive conversations into

00:04:55.694 --> 00:05:00.954
a hidden section protected by a four-digit pin or by your phone's fingerprint or face ID.

00:05:00.954 --> 00:05:04.954
A pin is just a secret number lock, like the code that wakes your phone.

00:05:05.614 --> 00:05:09.674
Once a chat is locked, it disappears from your normal history and from search results.

00:05:10.134 --> 00:05:12.874
So someone who borrows your phone will not stumble into it.

00:05:13.334 --> 00:05:18.294
That is useful if you journal, draft a personal story, or talk about private topics with

00:05:18.294 --> 00:05:18.674
the AI.

00:05:19.314 --> 00:05:22.794
Treat it like a diary with a clasp, sitting next to your ordinary notebooks.

